1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,800
Welcome back to Rashi in Brief, where each

2
00:00:02,864 --> 00:00:05,280
week we explore the Torah portion through the

3
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:08,720
eyes of the great medieval commentator, Rabbi Shlomo

4
00:00:08,853 --> 00:00:10,960
Yitzchaki, known as Rashi.

5
00:00:11,168 --> 00:00:14,000
This week we have a double portion called

6
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:19,920
Nitzavim-Vayeilech, found in Deuteronomy chapters twenty-nine through thirty-one,

7
00:00:19,940 --> 00:00:22,480
and Rashi has some truly remarkable things to

8
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:27,390
say about these final chapters of Moses's life.This

9
00:00:27,447 --> 00:00:30,350
double portion takes place on the very last

10
00:00:30,430 --> 00:00:31,950
day of Moses's life.

11
00:00:32,217 --> 00:00:35,470
He gathers the entire people of Israel, from

12
00:00:35,490 --> 00:00:37,870
the leaders down to the woodcutters and water

13
00:00:37,930 --> 00:00:40,830
drawers, and brings them into a covenant with

14
00:00:40,930 --> 00:00:41,310
God.

15
00:00:41,603 --> 00:00:45,310
He warns them about the consequences of idolatry,

16
00:00:45,350 --> 00:00:49,070
but he also offers them extraordinary hope, promising

17
00:00:49,102 --> 00:00:52,030
that no matter how far they stray, return

18
00:00:52,137 --> 00:00:53,470
is always possible.

19
00:00:53,817 --> 00:00:56,883
He then speaks about the accessibility of the

20
00:00:56,910 --> 00:01:00,750
Torah itself, famously declaring that it is not

21
00:01:00,830 --> 00:01:03,790
in heaven or across the sea but right

22
00:01:03,854 --> 00:01:05,310
here, close to us.

23
00:01:05,710 --> 00:01:10,590
In the second portion, Vayeilech, Moses formally transfers

24
00:01:10,634 --> 00:01:13,630
leadership to Joshua, instructs the people about a

25
00:01:13,687 --> 00:01:16,992
public Torah reading every seven years, and begins

26
00:01:17,048 --> 00:01:19,312
preparing them for a future without him.

27
00:01:19,436 --> 00:01:23,632
Throughout these chapters, Rashi is drawn to subtle

28
00:01:23,712 --> 00:01:28,912
word choices, grammatical oddities, and juxtapositions between sections.

29
00:01:29,179 --> 00:01:32,592
He asks why certain passages appear where they

30
00:01:32,725 --> 00:01:36,432
do, what unusual verb forms really mean, and

31
00:01:36,472 --> 00:01:39,952
how small details reveal large truths about God's

32
00:01:40,007 --> 00:01:44,836
relationship with Israel.Let's begin with the very opening

33
00:01:44,903 --> 00:01:46,276
words of the portion.

34
00:01:46,436 --> 00:01:50,196
The Torah says, "You are standing this day,

35
00:01:50,256 --> 00:01:52,756
all of you, before the Lord your God."

36
00:01:53,023 --> 00:01:55,796
Rashi asks a question that might not occur

37
00:01:55,849 --> 00:01:58,199
to us at first: why does the Torah

38
00:01:58,231 --> 00:02:00,359
need to say "this day"?

39
00:02:00,399 --> 00:02:02,679
And why is this passage placed right after

40
00:02:02,699 --> 00:02:05,559
the terrifying curses of the previous chapter?

41
00:02:05,852 --> 00:02:08,919
Rashi offers a beautiful answer.

42
00:02:09,106 --> 00:02:11,959
He says that when Israel heard those ninety-eight

43
00:02:12,050 --> 00:02:15,639
curses, their faces turned pale and they cried

44
00:02:15,719 --> 00:02:19,239
out, "Who can possibly withstand all of this?"

45
00:02:19,532 --> 00:02:22,359
So Moses began to calm them, saying in

46
00:02:22,416 --> 00:02:26,599
effect, "Look, you are standing here today!

47
00:02:26,639 --> 00:02:29,639
You have provoked God many times, and yet

48
00:02:29,692 --> 00:02:31,479
He has not destroyed you.

49
00:02:31,579 --> 00:02:35,239
You are still here." And then Rashi adds

50
00:02:35,279 --> 00:02:38,599
something poetic about the word "today," saying that

51
00:02:38,619 --> 00:02:41,639
the day is like Israel's existence: though it

52
00:02:41,706 --> 00:02:44,839
grows dark for a time, it shines again.

53
00:02:45,019 --> 00:02:49,159
The very curses and sufferings, paradoxically, are what

54
00:02:49,191 --> 00:02:51,879
give Israel their permanence and stability.

55
00:02:52,092 --> 00:02:55,399
It is a stunning reframing: what looked like

56
00:02:55,479 --> 00:03:00,821
a terrifying threat becomes a source of reassurance.Next,

57
00:03:00,881 --> 00:03:02,741
let's look at a phrase that seems like

58
00:03:02,781 --> 00:03:06,501
a minor detail but opens up something profound.

59
00:03:06,661 --> 00:03:09,863
The Torah lists all the people present at

60
00:03:09,895 --> 00:03:13,703
this covenant ceremony, ending with "from the woodcutter

61
00:03:13,836 --> 00:03:17,463
to the drawer of water." Why mention these

62
00:03:17,532 --> 00:03:19,783
humble laborers specifically?

63
00:03:20,076 --> 00:03:24,023
Rashi tells us this teaches that Canaanites came

64
00:03:24,076 --> 00:03:27,063
to Moses wanting to convert, just as the

65
00:03:27,143 --> 00:03:30,743
Gibeonites later came to Joshua through trickery.

66
00:03:30,916 --> 00:03:34,743
Moses accepted them but assigned them these roles

67
00:03:34,796 --> 00:03:37,303
as woodcutters and water drawers.

68
00:03:37,447 --> 00:03:40,583
What is remarkable here is the inclusiveness of

69
00:03:40,623 --> 00:03:44,663
the covenant: even these newcomers, people from outside

70
00:03:44,683 --> 00:03:48,263
the community, are standing before God and entering

71
00:03:48,283 --> 00:03:49,303
the covenant.

72
00:03:49,483 --> 00:03:51,943
And the Torah goes on to say that

73
00:03:51,991 --> 00:03:54,423
this covenant is made not only with those

74
00:03:54,493 --> 00:03:57,383
present but also with those "who are not

75
00:03:57,447 --> 00:04:01,383
here today." Rashi clarifies that this does not

76
00:04:01,447 --> 00:04:04,103
mean people who happened to be absent.

77
00:04:04,194 --> 00:04:07,863
Rather, it refers to all future generations.

78
00:04:07,983 --> 00:04:11,143
Every Jew who would ever live is included

79
00:04:11,196 --> 00:04:12,343
in this moment.

80
00:04:12,463 --> 00:04:15,383
The covenant is not just a historical event

81
00:04:15,463 --> 00:04:19,530
but an eternal bond.One of the most famous

82
00:04:19,599 --> 00:04:22,090
verses in the Torah appears in this portion:

83
00:04:22,530 --> 00:04:26,010
"It is not in heaven." Moses is speaking

84
00:04:26,063 --> 00:04:28,654
about the Torah and its commandments, and he

85
00:04:28,702 --> 00:04:32,174
says, "This commandment is not too wondrous for

86
00:04:32,234 --> 00:04:34,334
you, nor is it far off.

87
00:04:34,441 --> 00:04:36,334
It is not in heaven...

88
00:04:36,394 --> 00:04:38,574
nor is it beyond the sea...

89
00:04:38,634 --> 00:04:41,054
but the word is very near to you."

90
00:04:41,534 --> 00:04:44,894
Rashi makes two incisive comments here.

91
00:04:45,094 --> 00:04:48,654
First, he explains that the word usually translated

92
00:04:48,761 --> 00:04:53,134
as "too wondrous" or "too difficult," niflet, actually

93
00:04:53,214 --> 00:04:57,697
means "concealed" or "hidden." The Torah is not

94
00:04:57,743 --> 00:04:58,657
hidden from you.

95
00:04:58,817 --> 00:05:03,057
And then, with characteristic boldness, Rashi adds: even

96
00:05:03,084 --> 00:05:04,977
if it were in heaven, it would still

97
00:05:05,030 --> 00:05:07,057
be your duty to go up after it

98
00:05:07,117 --> 00:05:07,937
and learn it.

99
00:05:08,197 --> 00:05:12,017
The accessibility of Torah is not an excuse

100
00:05:12,037 --> 00:05:13,137
for complacency.

101
00:05:13,377 --> 00:05:15,857
It is a gift, but it also comes

102
00:05:15,873 --> 00:05:18,497
with an expectation: you have been given the

103
00:05:18,537 --> 00:05:21,621
Torah in writing and orally, so you have

104
00:05:21,728 --> 00:05:25,365
no excuse not to engage with it.Moving into

105
00:05:25,445 --> 00:05:29,206
Vayeilech, the Torah tells us, "And Moses went

106
00:05:29,246 --> 00:05:31,846
and spoke these words to all Israel.

107
00:05:32,286 --> 00:05:34,886
I am one hundred and twenty years old

108
00:05:34,966 --> 00:05:35,846
today.

109
00:05:35,886 --> 00:05:37,606
I can no longer go out and come

110
00:05:37,713 --> 00:05:40,166
in." Rashi is troubled.

111
00:05:40,366 --> 00:05:43,046
You might think Moses is saying his body

112
00:05:43,066 --> 00:05:45,766
has grown weak, but the Torah itself tells

113
00:05:45,846 --> 00:05:48,806
us later that when Moses died, "his eye

114
00:05:48,846 --> 00:05:51,927
was not dim nor his natural force abated."

115
00:05:52,299 --> 00:05:54,086
So what does he mean?

116
00:05:54,406 --> 00:05:57,126
Rashi offers two explanations.

117
00:05:57,299 --> 00:06:00,646
First, Moses means he is no longer permitted

118
00:06:00,726 --> 00:06:03,606
to lead, because authority has been transferred to

119
00:06:03,675 --> 00:06:04,166
Joshua.

120
00:06:04,426 --> 00:06:09,766
And second, Rashi says something deeply moving: Moses

121
00:06:09,826 --> 00:06:12,566
can no longer "go out and come in"

122
00:06:12,619 --> 00:06:15,449
in matters of Torah, because the traditions and

123
00:06:15,529 --> 00:06:18,489
wellsprings of wisdom were stopped up for him.

124
00:06:18,713 --> 00:06:22,649
Even for Moses, the greatest prophet, the end

125
00:06:22,676 --> 00:06:25,049
of his mission meant the closing of channels

126
00:06:25,076 --> 00:06:25,849
of insight.

127
00:06:26,041 --> 00:06:28,569
This is not physical decline.

128
00:06:28,809 --> 00:06:31,529
It is the recognition that his role is

129
00:06:31,600 --> 00:06:36,150
complete.There is a fascinating contrast Rashi draws between

130
00:06:36,182 --> 00:06:38,870
what Moses says to Joshua and what God

131
00:06:38,934 --> 00:06:39,830
says to Joshua.

132
00:06:40,057 --> 00:06:43,590
Moses tells Joshua, "You shall go with this

133
00:06:43,659 --> 00:06:46,870
people into the land." But God later tells

134
00:06:46,939 --> 00:06:50,070
Joshua, "You shall bring the children of Israel

135
00:06:50,150 --> 00:06:53,910
into the land." Rashi explains the difference with

136
00:06:53,972 --> 00:06:55,350
striking directness.

137
00:06:55,550 --> 00:06:58,950
Moses is saying: the elders of the generation

138
00:06:58,998 --> 00:07:02,310
will be with you; lead collaboratively, following their

139
00:07:02,390 --> 00:07:03,030
counsel.

140
00:07:03,250 --> 00:07:07,030
But God is saying something very different: you

141
00:07:07,083 --> 00:07:10,270
shall bring them even against their will.

142
00:07:10,443 --> 00:07:13,270
If necessary, take a stick and beat them

143
00:07:13,318 --> 00:07:14,150
over the head.

144
00:07:14,270 --> 00:07:16,710
There can be only one leader for a

145
00:07:16,783 --> 00:07:18,710
generation, not two.

146
00:07:18,966 --> 00:07:23,510
This tension between collaborative and decisive leadership is

147
00:07:23,574 --> 00:07:26,470
left standing in the text, and Rashi lets

148
00:07:26,523 --> 00:07:30,227
us feel its full force.Finally, there is a

149
00:07:30,289 --> 00:07:31,987
poignant comment near the end.

150
00:07:32,174 --> 00:07:36,147
Moses says, "After my death you will utterly

151
00:07:36,207 --> 00:07:39,267
corrupt yourselves." But we know from the book

152
00:07:39,320 --> 00:07:42,067
of Judges that the people actually served God

153
00:07:42,125 --> 00:07:44,307
faithfully throughout the days of Joshua.

154
00:07:44,574 --> 00:07:47,667
So how could Moses say corruption would follow

155
00:07:47,727 --> 00:07:49,347
immediately after his death?

156
00:07:49,720 --> 00:07:53,907
Rashi's answer is tender: a person's student should

157
00:07:53,934 --> 00:07:55,507
be as dear to him as his own

158
00:07:55,619 --> 00:07:56,147
self.

159
00:07:56,547 --> 00:07:59,187
As long as Joshua lived, it was as

160
00:07:59,221 --> 00:08:01,587
though Moses himself were still alive.

161
00:08:01,747 --> 00:08:05,027
When Moses said "after my death," he was

162
00:08:05,091 --> 00:08:07,907
including Joshua's lifetime as an extension of his

163
00:08:08,007 --> 00:08:13,601
own.Stepping back, several themes run through Rashi's commentary

164
00:08:13,654 --> 00:08:15,041
on this double portion.

165
00:08:15,188 --> 00:08:19,281
There is the theme of reassurance: even after

166
00:08:19,354 --> 00:08:23,681
devastating curses, God's relationship with Israel endures.

167
00:08:23,814 --> 00:08:27,685
There is the theme of accessibility: Torah is

168
00:08:27,745 --> 00:08:31,125
not hidden or distant but given directly into

169
00:08:31,205 --> 00:08:32,245
human hands.

170
00:08:32,392 --> 00:08:35,765
There is the theme of leadership and transition:

171
00:08:35,893 --> 00:08:38,725
what it means to pass the mantle, and

172
00:08:38,765 --> 00:08:42,165
how different styles of leadership serve different moments.

173
00:08:42,405 --> 00:08:45,125
And there is the theme of covenant as

174
00:08:45,205 --> 00:08:48,485
eternal: binding not just those who stood at

175
00:08:48,533 --> 00:08:52,165
that assembly but every generation to come.

176
00:08:52,230 --> 00:08:55,845
Throughout it all, Rashi shows us his characteristic

177
00:08:55,902 --> 00:08:59,925
method: he notices a word that seems unnecessary,

178
00:09:00,125 --> 00:09:02,485
a phrase that could have been written differently,

179
00:09:02,725 --> 00:09:06,245
a passage placed in an unexpected location, and

180
00:09:06,293 --> 00:09:09,765
from these small observations he draws out meanings

181
00:09:09,797 --> 00:09:12,885
that are at once precise and expansive.

182
00:09:13,205 --> 00:09:16,725
He teaches us that careful reading is itself

183
00:09:16,778 --> 00:09:21,224
an act of devotion.Thank you so much for

184
00:09:21,274 --> 00:09:24,104
joining me on Rashi in Brief this week.

185
00:09:24,324 --> 00:09:27,224
May the words of this portion remind us

186
00:09:27,344 --> 00:09:31,144
all that return is always possible, that wisdom

187
00:09:31,224 --> 00:09:34,984
is always near, and that every generation stands

188
00:09:35,037 --> 00:09:37,304
in that same eternal covenant.

189
00:09:37,477 --> 00:09:40,104
Until next week, shalom.

